<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bulawayo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bulawayo is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, in the south-west of the country. It began in the 1870s as the royal town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobengula">Lobengula</a>, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a>, and was rebuilt by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> after its occupation in 1893. It grew into a major railway and industrial centre. The ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khami">Khami</a>, a capital of the Torwa state, lie nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bulawayo.jpg" alt="Bulawayo" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prince Phumulani Nyoni · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bulawayo_CBD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bulawayo is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>'s Matabeleland region. It covers 546 square kilometres in the west of the country along the Matsheumhlope River, and most of its people belong to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a>, with minorities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a> and others. The city lies close to Matobo National Park and the Khami World Heritage Site.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The settlement began as a kraal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mzilikazi">Mzilikazi</a>, who led his people north from Kwazulu. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobengula">Lobengula</a> succeeded him in the 1860s and ruled from Bulawayo until 1893. The name comes from the Ndebele word bulala and is translated as the one to be killed. Many European powers coveted the town, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a> and the Chartered Company to avoid alarming its rivals. Lobengula once described Britain as a chameleon and himself as the fly.</p><p>In the First Matabele War of 1893, troops of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> invaded and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobengula">Lobengula</a> to leave, after he had blown up munitions and set the town on fire. On 4 November 1893 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leander-starr-jameson">Leander Starr Jameson</a> declared Bulawayo a settlement under company rule, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> ordered it founded on the ruins of the royal kraal, where State House now stands. During the Second Matabele War the town was besieged from March 1896. Settlers sheltered in a laager, and more than 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> warriors gathered but never mounted a significant attack. Relief forces arrived in late May 1896.</p><p>Bulawayo became a municipality in 1897 and a city in 1943. By the late 1930s it was no longer the country's largest city, as activity shifted east towards Salisbury, but after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it grew again as an industrial centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bulawayo is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>'s second largest city, and a hub of the national rail network and headquarters of the National Railways of Zimbabwe. Its wide central streets were designed so that ox-drawn carts could turn in them. The National University of Science and Technology and the Natural History Museum of Zimbabwe are in the city, which is nicknamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">City of Kings</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bulawayo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1980s Derek Hudson, conductor of the <b>Bulawayo</b> Philharmonic Orchestra, needed long negotiations with the authorities before he could give Zimbabwe's first performance of Beethoven's Ninth Symphony, and at Christmas 1994 the tune in a fundraising organ recital at a Harare church provoked angry protests.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rise-o-voices-of-rhodesia">Rise, O Voices of Rhodesia</a></li><li><blockquote>Butua arose in the mid-15th century as the Kingdom of Zimbabwe declined, when the Torwa dynasty took control of the south-western plateau around modern <b>Bulawayo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-butua">Kingdom of Butua</a></li><li><blockquote>Lobengula, who succeeded in 1870, built his capital at <b>Bulawayo</b> and tried to balance concession-seekers, but the 1888 Rudd Concession opened the way to Cecil Rhodes.</blockquote><a href="/state/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The railway from <b>Bulawayo</b> crossed the Victoria Falls bridge in 1905 and reached the Broken Hill mine in 1906.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-western-rhodesia">North-Western Rhodesia</a></li></ul></section>
